f 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308066 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308066 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308066 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ab070b56-038b-599b-b680-8ef2d02605e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308092 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308092 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308092 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308092 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186305415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308092 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b4f564b5-049c-5bd7-a511-bc72af30b334" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308144 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308144 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308144 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308144 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308144 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308144 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308144 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308144 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186307507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308144 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c7482acd-6813-50d9-954d-0ffe427a7d23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308216 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308216 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308216 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308216 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308216 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308216 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308216 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308216 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308216 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dd4d4697-8b4c-5997-bfc4-9a5ec2b0f3a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308641 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308641 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308641 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308641 #edb/ref 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.18,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.18)=0.486.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.006.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308641 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f94b6c1-fa0e-5c48-970a-a0d605d1856d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308641 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.18$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.18}{0.06}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.486$ to $0.006.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.486}{0.006}=81.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈81 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(81) \\\\ p & ≈4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate order of the method is $4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308663 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308663 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334644" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308663 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308663 #edb/ref 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.25)=0.50.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.01.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308663 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c9e60f41-0074-5df6-9afc-69a24a0bbeb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308663 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(2.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.50$ to $0.01.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.50}{0.01}=50.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.5)^{p+1} & ≈50 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(50) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.269… \\\\ p & ≈3.269….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308667 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308667 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334650" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308667 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308667 #edb/ref 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.20,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.20)=0.78.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.008.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308667 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f92b889e-ac1a-5f7b-b100-034485795a76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308667 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.20$ to $0.08.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.20}{0.08}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(2.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.78$ to $0.008.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.78}{0.008}=97.5.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.5)^{p+1} & ≈97.5 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(97.5) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.99… \\\\ p & ≈3.99….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308676 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308676 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308676 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308676 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$\n- $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6}$\n- $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308676 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "512c526c-f958-5c94-ad6a-b60bd207799d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308676 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{4}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $11(Δx)^{5}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $5$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.2Δx.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308686 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308686 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334781" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308686 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308686 #edb/ref 1071 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$\nSuppose that using step size $Δx=0.1$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.1)≈0.512.$\nIf we instead use a step size $Δx=0.025,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.025)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308686 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e9fb99c-0502-586a-b64c-76d5a0146e7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308686 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.1$ to $0.025.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.1}{0.025}=4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4}={4}^{4}=256.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.025,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.025)≈\\frac{0.512}{256}=0.002.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308710 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308710 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334947" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308710 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308710 #edb/ref 1071 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.4$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.4)≈1.024.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308710 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cfc83ddc-db4f-5e46-adbe-84eb415a3d1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308710 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.4}{0.1}=4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4+1}={4}^{5}=1024.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{1.024}{1024}=0.001.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
